Phase 1: Planning & Architecture (Days 1–3)
Before writing code, define the "Skeleton" of your application.
1. The Tech Stack
•	Frontend: React.js (Vite for faster builds).
•	Styling: Tailwind CSS (for rapid UI development).
•	State Management: Zustand or Redux Toolkit (Zustand is recommended for a 1-month timeline due to less boilerplate).
•	Routing: React Router DOM.
•	Backend/BaaS: Firebase (Auth, Firestore, Storage) or Supabase for a "no-backend" approach.
•	Payments: Stripe API (Test Mode).
2. Folder Structure
Organize your project to stay scalable:
Plaintext
src/
├── assets/          # Images, icons, logos
├── components/      # Reusable UI (Buttons, Inputs, Navbar)
├── features/        # Feature-specific logic (Cart, ProductList)
├── hooks/           # Custom hooks (useAuth, useLocalStorage)
├── pages/           # Main views (Home, ProductDetail, Checkout)
├── store/           # Global state (Zustand/Redux)
└── utils/           # Helper functions (Currency formatters)
________________________________________


Phase 2: Core UI & Routing (Days 4–10)
Build the visual shells and the navigation logic.
Essential Pages & Functions
•	Home Page: Hero section, Featured Products, and Categories.
•	Product Listing Page (PLP): Grid layout with Filtering (price, category) and Sorting (low-to-high).
•	Product Detail Page (PDP): Image gallery, price, description, and "Add to Cart" button.
•	Cart Drawer/Page: List of items, quantity toggles, and subtotal calculation.
•	Authentication: Sign Up/Login forms with validation (Formik or React Hook Form).
________________________________________
Phase 3: State Management & Logic (Days 11–20)
This is where the app starts "working."
1. Global Cart Logic
You need a central store to handle:
•	addItem: Check if item exists (increment quantity) or add new.
•	removeItem: Filter out the item ID.
•	updateQuantity: Increase/Decrease limits.
•	Persistence: Use localStorage so the cart survives a page refresh.
2. Authentication Integration
•	Connect your forms to Firebase/Supabase.
•	Create Protected Routes (e.g., a user shouldn't see the "Order History" page if they aren't logged in).
3. Product Data Fetching
•	Use useEffect or TanStack Query (React Query) to fetch data from an API (like FakeStoreAPI) or your own database.
________________________________________
Phase 4: Checkout & Payments (Days 21–25)
The most critical part of e-commerce.
The Checkout Flow:
1.	Shipping Form: Collect address and contact info.
2.	Order Summary: Final check of items and taxes.
3.	Payment Processing: * Integrate Stripe Elements.
•	Handle the "Payment Intent" on the server/serverless function.
•	Success/Failure feedback screens.
________________________________________
Phase 5: Optimization & Deployment (Days 26–30)
1. Performance & UX
•	Skeleton Screens: Show placeholders while products load.
•	Image Optimization: Use modern formats (WebP) and lazy loading.
•	Responsive Design: Ensure the mobile shopping experience is flawless.
2. Deployment
•	Hosting: Vercel or Netlify (highly optimized for React).
•	Environment Variables: Secure your API keys (.env files).
________________________________________
Checklist of "Pro" Features to Include:
•	Search Bar: Real-time filtering of products.
•	Wishlist: A "Save for later" feature.
•	Toasts/Notifications: Pop-up alerts when an item is added to the cart.
•	Dark Mode: A nice-to-have for modern UI.
Pro-Tip: Don't try to build a custom backend from scratch if you only have 30 days. Use Firebase or Supabase to handle the database and auth so you can spend 90% of your time perfecting the React frontend.




